WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:01.922
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:01.922 --> 00:00:03.880
LARRY P. ARNN: The problem
with education today

00:00:03.880 --> 00:00:07.240
is that it's abandoned
the ground of education.

00:00:09.910 --> 00:00:13.150
Students today are taught
that the truth is according

00:00:13.150 --> 00:00:16.780
to the perspective of one,
that things in history

00:00:16.780 --> 00:00:18.500
are not fixed--

00:00:18.500 --> 00:00:21.020
that because we see
them differently,

00:00:21.020 --> 00:00:22.997
they are different.

00:00:22.997 --> 00:00:24.700
DAVID DIENER: Most
education today

00:00:24.700 --> 00:00:27.132
assumes that the student
is the starting point.

00:00:27.132 --> 00:00:29.590
KENNETH CALVERT: There's a
great deal of self-centeredness.

00:00:29.590 --> 00:00:32.740
There's a lack of civility,
a lot of shouting,

00:00:32.740 --> 00:00:36.468
and a lack of real
thoughtful interaction.

00:00:36.468 --> 00:00:38.260
LARRY P. ARNN: So that
means the only thing

00:00:38.260 --> 00:00:40.900
wrong with education today is
that it's lost its mission.

00:00:40.900 --> 00:00:43.972
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:43.972 --> 00:00:45.430
DAVID DIENER: I've
become convinced

00:00:45.430 --> 00:00:49.870
that one of the greatest ways
that I can impact the future

00:00:49.870 --> 00:00:52.960
is by investing in
classical education

00:00:52.960 --> 00:00:55.060
and helping to
cultivate students

00:00:55.060 --> 00:00:57.190
into men and women
who are able to live

00:00:57.190 --> 00:01:00.280
virtuous and wise lives.

00:01:00.280 --> 00:01:03.220
KENNETH CALVERT: The
classical model is not dead.

00:01:03.220 --> 00:01:06.601
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:01:07.792 --> 00:01:09.250
DAVID DIENER: At
Hillsdale Academy,

00:01:09.250 --> 00:01:12.100
our motto is
[SPEAKING LATIN],, which

00:01:12.100 --> 00:01:14.770
is Latin for virtue and wisdom.

00:01:14.770 --> 00:01:17.770
And that really is the
backbone of what we do here.

00:01:17.770 --> 00:01:20.740
And it's really the backbone of
what a classical education is

00:01:20.740 --> 00:01:22.582
all about.

00:01:22.582 --> 00:01:24.040
KATHLEEN O'TOOLE:
In America today,

00:01:24.040 --> 00:01:26.860
I think we often
think of education

00:01:26.860 --> 00:01:29.200
as training for a
particular career

00:01:29.200 --> 00:01:31.640
or a particular course of life.

00:01:31.640 --> 00:01:35.170
A classical education is
more well-rounded than that,

00:01:35.170 --> 00:01:38.020
and it remembers that,
no matter who you are,

00:01:38.020 --> 00:01:40.630
there's a moral
education, a character

00:01:40.630 --> 00:01:42.047
education, a civic education.

00:01:42.047 --> 00:01:43.630
DAVID DIENER: And
so, what we're doing

00:01:43.630 --> 00:01:45.730
in the classical
education tradition is,

00:01:45.730 --> 00:01:48.560
we are drawing on the
wisdom of the ages.

00:01:48.560 --> 00:01:51.550
We are exposing our
students to the wisdom

00:01:51.550 --> 00:01:52.475
that has gone before.

00:01:52.475 --> 00:01:55.506
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:01:56.025 --> 00:01:57.850
So we don't get
to make up truth.

00:01:57.850 --> 00:02:00.940
Just as there are laws that
govern the physical universe,

00:02:00.940 --> 00:02:03.230
there are also moral laws.

00:02:03.230 --> 00:02:05.650
ELIZABETH SCHLUETER: One of
the challenges of education

00:02:05.650 --> 00:02:10.280
is forming virtue
in its students.

00:02:10.280 --> 00:02:12.520
And this is something
the Academy excels at.

00:02:12.520 --> 00:02:14.920
ELIZABETH GRZESIAK:
Things like fortitude,

00:02:14.920 --> 00:02:18.940
perseverance, prudence--

00:02:18.940 --> 00:02:23.770
these good virtues are tested
at school on a daily basis.

00:02:23.770 --> 00:02:26.350
I learned the importance
of responsibility

00:02:26.350 --> 00:02:30.490
and being able to take care
of the people around me.

00:02:30.490 --> 00:02:33.843
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:02:36.140 --> 00:02:37.890
DAVID DIENER: From
Plato's Academy onward,

00:02:37.890 --> 00:02:41.390
over the centuries, learning
has happened in community.

00:02:41.390 --> 00:02:43.130
And what we have at
Hillsdale Academy

00:02:43.130 --> 00:02:44.840
is a community of learners.

00:02:44.840 --> 00:02:47.900
Our students, our teachers,
our staff-- everyone

00:02:47.900 --> 00:02:51.590
working together to promote
the cultivation of virtue

00:02:51.590 --> 00:02:54.525
and wisdom in our students.

00:02:54.525 --> 00:02:56.150
NATHAN SCHLUETER:
Our daughter, Helen--

00:02:56.150 --> 00:03:00.680
in a given semester, she's
learning Shakespearean drama,

00:03:00.680 --> 00:03:04.820
competed in cross country,
played violin in the orchestra.

00:03:04.820 --> 00:03:08.540
This was not a girl
that was stressed out

00:03:08.540 --> 00:03:12.425
about filling up a
resume with activities

00:03:12.425 --> 00:03:15.050
that she could burnish a resume
with to get into their college.

00:03:15.050 --> 00:03:19.790
She wanted to-- she was
just loving her friendships

00:03:19.790 --> 00:03:21.330
and all of these things.

00:03:21.330 --> 00:03:25.460
And I just see that
and think, wow.

00:03:25.460 --> 00:03:27.560
RACHEL CUTHBERT: It
has truly been a joy

00:03:27.560 --> 00:03:30.325
to watch our children
learn to love to learn.

00:03:30.325 --> 00:03:31.700
NATHAN SCHLUETER:
There hardly is

00:03:31.700 --> 00:03:33.890
a day that goes
by that we are not

00:03:33.890 --> 00:03:39.440
looking at each other with a
kind of giddiness of gratitude

00:03:39.440 --> 00:03:41.072
for having the Academy here.

00:03:41.072 --> 00:03:42.530
DEANNA DUCHER: For
this tiny school

00:03:42.530 --> 00:03:46.790
that has had 303 graduates
for 21 years straight,

00:03:46.790 --> 00:03:50.840
we had at least one student
in the national merit program.

00:03:50.840 --> 00:03:52.610
There was one year
that hit a class--

00:03:52.610 --> 00:03:56.090
I think, of '19, we had five
national merit finalists.

00:03:56.090 --> 00:03:58.190
NOLAN SULLIVAN: I had
three different teachers

00:03:58.190 --> 00:04:01.340
in high school who have their
PhD, which, I feel like,

00:04:01.340 --> 00:04:02.780
is very rare.

00:04:02.780 --> 00:04:04.490
MICHAEL ROBERTS:
This is not a place

00:04:04.490 --> 00:04:06.050
that teachers pass through.

00:04:06.050 --> 00:04:07.062
It's a destination.

00:04:07.062 --> 00:04:08.270
It's where they want to stay.

00:04:08.270 --> 00:04:10.062
It's where they want
to build their career,

00:04:10.062 --> 00:04:13.220
put down their roots, and
really invest long-term

00:04:13.220 --> 00:04:15.618
in Hillsdale Academy,
in Hillsdale College,

00:04:15.618 --> 00:04:16.535
and in their students.

00:04:16.535 --> 00:04:19.730
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:04:22.140 --> 00:04:23.980
KENNETH CALVERT: It's
not an elite school.

00:04:23.980 --> 00:04:26.220
It's not a prep school.

00:04:26.220 --> 00:04:30.540
What it is is a school
that holds the bar high so

00:04:30.540 --> 00:04:33.240
that we can produce the
best sorts of citizens

00:04:33.240 --> 00:04:35.670
that our country needs.

00:04:35.670 --> 00:04:37.170
ELIZABETH GRZESIAK:
So it's not just

00:04:37.170 --> 00:04:39.420
about our little
school in Hillsdale.

00:04:39.420 --> 00:04:42.480
Suddenly, it's quite big.

00:04:42.480 --> 00:04:47.640
This is for the
sake of our nation,

00:04:47.640 --> 00:04:53.310
for the sake of just
government and self governance.

00:04:53.310 --> 00:04:56.070
Everything that our
country was founded on.

00:04:56.070 --> 00:04:59.730
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:04:59.730 --> 00:05:02.070
DAVID DIENER: As
Hillsdale College's work

00:05:02.070 --> 00:05:05.760
in K-12 classical education
continues to expand,

00:05:05.760 --> 00:05:08.550
the Academy will continue
to play an incredibly

00:05:08.550 --> 00:05:11.220
important role in that mission.

00:05:11.220 --> 00:05:13.830
We want to continue
growing as a school,

00:05:13.830 --> 00:05:16.890
becoming better at the
education we provide,

00:05:16.890 --> 00:05:19.410
continuing to expand
our facilities to meet

00:05:19.410 --> 00:05:22.380
the needs of our student
enrollment growth

00:05:22.380 --> 00:05:26.340
so that we can better serve the
mission of Hillsdale College--

00:05:26.340 --> 00:05:28.110
and serve our country--

00:05:28.110 --> 00:05:33.240
as a model of what classical
education can and should be.

00:05:33.240 --> 00:05:36.040
LARRY P. ARNN: The Academy
is an excellent thing.

00:05:36.040 --> 00:05:38.020
And it needs to be.

00:05:38.020 --> 00:05:39.930
And so, for it to be
an excellent thing,

00:05:39.930 --> 00:05:43.380
it needs to be funded
to a sufficient level.

00:05:43.380 --> 00:05:45.520
Education for the young--

00:05:45.520 --> 00:05:48.480
it's for people who are
not making a living yet.

00:05:48.480 --> 00:05:50.550
Somebody has to help them.

00:05:50.550 --> 00:05:53.600
And so, that partnership between
the people who support it

00:05:53.600 --> 00:05:56.210
and the people who carry
on its activity, that's

00:05:56.210 --> 00:05:58.980
natural and necessary.

00:05:58.980 --> 00:06:01.410
KATHLEEN O'TOOLE: By
supporting Hillsdale Academy,

00:06:01.410 --> 00:06:06.450
you are helping
Hillsdale College teach K

00:06:06.450 --> 00:06:09.450
through 12 schools
across the country

00:06:09.450 --> 00:06:12.060
what a beautiful classical
education looks like

00:06:12.060 --> 00:06:14.720
and how education in this
country ought to be done.

00:06:14.720 --> 00:06:17.673
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:06:17.673 --> 00:06:19.590
DAVID DIENER: We're not
in this to make money.

00:06:19.590 --> 00:06:22.860
We are in this to
serve the students who

00:06:22.860 --> 00:06:26.350
walk the halls of our buildings,
to bless their families--

00:06:26.350 --> 00:06:27.990
but also, to bless
and contribute

00:06:27.990 --> 00:06:30.300
to our nation and the world.

00:06:30.300 --> 00:06:34.800
ELIZABETH SCHLUETER: We are
very aware that the donors have

00:06:34.800 --> 00:06:38.250
supported that education
for our families.

00:06:38.250 --> 00:06:40.140
ELIZABETH GRZESIAK: I
was sponsored myself

00:06:40.140 --> 00:06:42.870
by a woman in
Florida who decided

00:06:42.870 --> 00:06:46.230
that classical
education was important.

00:06:46.230 --> 00:06:50.250
Because of Mrs. McCullom, I
was able to go to high school.

00:06:50.250 --> 00:06:53.508
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:06:53.508 --> 00:06:56.050
NOLAN SULLIVAN: Through coaching
basketball, coaching soccer,

00:06:56.050 --> 00:06:58.540
and doing tutoring, I've
still kept a connection

00:06:58.540 --> 00:06:59.920
to the school.

00:06:59.920 --> 00:07:02.170
And I think what
keeps drawing me back

00:07:02.170 --> 00:07:06.400
is, I've started to realize
more and more what Hillsdale

00:07:06.400 --> 00:07:10.920
Academy has given to me
and how much of an impact

00:07:10.920 --> 00:07:12.823
Hillsdale Academy
has had on my life.

00:07:12.823 --> 00:07:14.740
DAVID DIENER: The reach
that Hillsdale Academy

00:07:14.740 --> 00:07:18.700
has had across our nation
and the educational impact

00:07:18.700 --> 00:07:19.480
is enormous.

00:07:19.480 --> 00:07:20.980
NATHAN SCHLUETER:
It's got a mission

00:07:20.980 --> 00:07:25.770
to prepare citizens
to be entrepreneurs

00:07:25.770 --> 00:07:29.370
and to raise families and to
help with other organizations.

00:07:29.370 --> 00:07:32.310
Because that's the
backbone of a free society.

00:07:32.310 --> 00:07:35.466
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:07:39.750 --> 00:07:41.880
DAVID DIENER: I can
think of no better way

00:07:41.880 --> 00:07:44.670
to invest in the future
than by investing

00:07:44.670 --> 00:07:48.360
in the classical education
of our young men and women.

00:07:48.360 --> 00:07:51.960
What classical schools like
Hillsdale Academy are doing

00:07:51.960 --> 00:07:56.100
is cultivating students so
that they can live well and so

00:07:56.100 --> 00:07:58.800
that they can pass
on what they have

00:07:58.800 --> 00:08:01.140
learned to future generations.

00:08:01.140 --> 00:08:05.130
A classical Christian education
is an investment in eternity.

00:08:05.130 --> 00:08:08.480
[MUSIC PLAYING]

